\section{Equations behind the added physics}

\subsection{Transport on the frozen helium field}
The steady carrier provides $\rho$, $\mathbf u$, $T$ and $p$. Each trace
gas is advanced with
\begin{equation}
 \underbrace{\frac{\partial(\rho Y_i)}{\partial t}}_{\text{storage}}
 +\underbrace{\nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf uY_i)}_{\text{advection}}
 =\underbrace{\nabla\cdot(\rho D_i\nabla Y_i)}_{\text{diffusion}}
 +S_i^{\rm rel}+S_i^{\rm wall}.
 \label{eq:transport}
\end{equation}
$Y_i$ is species mass per unit helium-carrier mass; $D_i$ is the molecular
diffusivity. The two sources represent release from the boat and exchange
with the wall, in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$. The reactive $Y_i$ do not
have to sum to one. Their evolution does not feed back into carrier
momentum or temperature. This is the trace-species approximation, and it
is least secure close to a strongly evaporating boat.

\subsection{From a prescribed onset to reversible exchange}
The temperature model activates deposition using an imposed threshold.
The HKS model instead compares the local vapour pressure with the
equilibrium pressure of the condensed phase:
\begin{equation}
 j_i=G_i[p_{{\rm eq},i}(T_w)-p_{i,w}],\qquad
 G_i=C_e s_i\frac{2\alpha_i}{2-\alpha_i}
       \sqrt{\frac{M_i}{2\pi RT_w}}.
 \label{eq:hks}
\end{equation}
Here $j_i$ is mass flux in $\mathrm{kg\,m^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$, positive
\emph{from the deposit into the gas}; $G_i$ is in $\mathrm{s/m}$.
$M_i$ is molar mass in $\mathrm{kg/mol}$, $R$ the gas constant,
$T_w$ interface temperature in K, and both pressures are in Pa.
$\alpha_i$ is accommodation; $C_e$ and $s_i$ are dimensionless
multipliers. The physical baseline sets all three to one.

If $p_{i,w}>p_{{\rm eq},i}$, then $j_i<0$ and vapour condenses.
If $p_{i,w}<p_{{\rm eq},i}$, then $j_i>0$ and existing material evaporates.
A bare wall cannot evaporate: release is capped by the available inventory.
The equilibrium-pressure table selects the stable solid or liquid branch.
Thus deposition temperature is an \emph{output}, rather than an imposed
threshold. The pressure law alone does not determine the profile: the gas
must also reach the surface by diffusion.

\subsection{Connecting the cell centre to the wall}
The finite-volume unknown is $Y_{i,c}$ at the cell centre, not the wall
pressure in Eq.~\eqref{eq:hks}. Define
$\chi_{i,c}=\rho_cRT_c/M_i$, so that $p_{i,c}=\chi_{i,c}Y_{i,c}$.
With wall distance $d_f$ and face diffusivity $(\rho D_i)_f$, eliminating
the unresolved wall concentration gives
\begin{equation}
 \frac{1}{G_{i,f}^{\rm eff}}=\frac{1}{G_i}
       +\frac{\chi_{i,c}d_f}{(\rho D_i)_f},\qquad
 j_{i,f}=G_{i,f}^{\rm eff}
       [p_{{\rm eq},i}(T_w)-\chi_{i,c}Y_{i,c}].
 \label{eq:resistance}
\end{equation}
The two terms are kinetic and half-cell diffusion resistances in series.
Even infinitely fast surface kinetics leave a finite diffusion limit.
This is a connection to the resolved gas field, not an extra fitted
sticking parameter. Case 016 checks precisely this change.

In that synthetic case the diffusion conductance is $D/d_f=1$\,m/s,
against a kinetic velocity $G_i\chi_{i,c}/\rho_c=63.7$--107\,m/s.
The combined value is only 0.985--0.991\,m/s. Yet the 1\,\% onset
moves by just 0.005\,K, while the peak drops by 7.4\,\% and moves one
bin downstream. An unchanged onset therefore does not imply an unchanged
deposition profile.

\subsection{Meaning of the plug-flow reference}
Here ``plug flow'' denotes an idealised, cross-sectionally uniform gas
stream, not a two-phase flow regime. The reference calculations follow
cooling and saturation without resolving the radial velocity or
concentration fields. For case 014, the onset reference is the temperature
where the imposed vapour partial pressure meets the equilibrium vapour
pressure. The multispecies reference additionally follows equilibrium
with fractional removal of condensate. These are simplified numerical
checks, not a second CFD solution.

The CFD model transports dilute vapour in helium and stores condensed
material in stationary wall inventories. It includes phase change, but
does not solve two moving phases: there are no transported droplets,
aerosol particles or flowing liquid films.

\clearpage
\subsection{Conservation during wall exchange}
For a single gas--condensate pair, the same realised face flux updates
both inventories:
\begin{equation}
 S_{i,c}^{\rm wall}=\frac{1}{V_c}\sum_{f\in c}A_fj_{i,f},\qquad
 \Delta m_{i,f}^{\rm wall}=-\Delta t\,A_fj_{i,f}.
 \label{eq:pair}
\end{equation}
$A_f$ is wall-face area and $V_c$ cell volume. The flux used here is the
one actually realised by the gas solve after inventory bounds have been
applied. Independently updating the wall with a predicted flux would not
guarantee cancellation.

Once reactions change molecular identities, conservation must be checked
for each element $k\in\{\mathrm{Pb},\mathrm{Bi},\mathrm I\}$:
\begin{equation}
 N_k^{0}+N_k^{\rm rel}+N_k^{\rm in}
 -N_k^{\rm gas}-N_k^{\rm wall}-N_k^{\rm out}=0.
 \label{eq:ledger}
\end{equation}
All $N_k$ are amounts of the element in mol; inlet and outlet terms
include the accounted boundary transport. The ledger reports the residual
relative to the case's reference inventory. Round-off closure verifies
bookkeeping of the represented processes. It does not establish the
accuracy of transport, thermodynamic data or experimental assumptions.

\subsection{Gas equilibrium preserves atoms, not molecules}
For gas species $i$ with $\nu_{ki}$ atoms of element $k$, local element
concentrations are
\begin{equation}
 b_k=\sum_i\nu_{ki}\frac{\rho Y_i}{M_i}.
 \label{eq:elements}
\end{equation}
The equilibrium step redistributes those atoms between the nine gases
Pb, PbI, PbI$_2$, Bi, Bi$_2$, BiI, BiI$_3$, I and I$_2$:
\begin{equation}
 \frac{p_i}{p^\circ}=K_{f,i}(T)
  \exp\!\left(\sum_k\nu_{ki}\lambda_k\right),\qquad
 \sum_i\nu_{ki}\frac{p_i}{RT}=b_k,
 \quad p^\circ=10^5\,\mathrm{Pa}.
 \label{eq:equilibrium}
\end{equation}
$K_{f,i}$ comes from the thermodynamic tables and the dimensionless
element potentials $\lambda_k$ are solved to satisfy the atom balances.
The conversion back is $Y_i=M_ip_i/(\rho RT)$. This assumes instantaneous
gas equilibrium, not measured reaction kinetics. In the implemented model
the redistribution is requested at $T\ge350$\,K; colder cells retain the
transported composition. Homogeneous nucleation and aerosols are excluded.

Case 021 also adds shared metal deposits and the forward wall channel
\begin{equation}
 3\mathrm{BiI}(g)\longrightarrow
 2\mathrm{Bi}(\mathrm{condensed})+\mathrm{BiI}_3(g).
\end{equation}
Both sides contain three Bi and three I atoms. Different channels draw
on the same physical Bi inventory. The forward-only channel is a modelling
choice; it should not be read as a complete reversible surface mechanism.

\subsection{OpenFOAM--GEMS3K coupling \hfill\normalfont\small\color{PSIgray}Jan Kren}
Jan implemented the optional GEMS3K coupling in the OpenFOAM solver through
a compiled bridge library. OpenFOAM advances transport and manages the
gas and wall inventories; GEMS3K supplies thermodynamic information.
The adapter maps species and elements, passes temperature, pressure and
composition, and checks returned states before they are used.

There are two distinct uses. In case 015, local GEMS3K calculations supply
$p_{{\rm eq},i}$ for the HKS wall and boat exchange. OpenFOAM then evaluates
the exchange flux and updates both inventories conservatively. Temperature,
dilution, convergence and table-deviation guards select the tabulated
pressure when a GEMS result is unsuitable.

In case 020, GEMS3K supplies gas standard states. The \emph{frozen} mode
caches formation constants at the prescribed carrier conditions and uses
the element-conserving equilibrium kernel each step. The \emph{local} mode
also calls GEMS3K per cell and step with a saved initial state; the exact
kernel refines the result and provides fallback, enforcing the transported
element amounts. Chemistry is therefore coupled sequentially to transport,
with no feedback to the frozen helium flow. Cases 015 and 020 verify these
optional paths; the main benchmark chain does not require a live GEMS3K
call. This coupling is Jan's implementation contribution, distinct from
the existing OpenFOAM and GEMS3K software.

