\section{The case ladder}

A model like this has many ingredients: the gas flow, heat, variable gas
properties, several chemical species, wall chemistry, gas-phase chemistry,
and the real geometry and temperatures of each experiment. If you add them
all at once and the answer is wrong, you do not know which ingredient is to
blame. So we did the opposite. The folder \texttt{run/} of the LESTO
repository holds 24 \emph{cases}, numbered 001 to 024, and every case obeys
the same four rules:

\begin{enumerate}[itemsep=0pt,label=\textbf{\color{PSIblue}\arabic*.}]
  \item Its \texttt{readme.md} starts with \emph{``This case is based on case N''}.
  \item It changes \textbf{one thing}, and shows the changed lines.
  \item It lists the \textbf{numbers to check} (e.g.\ ``mass closure below $10^{-15}$'').
  \item It says how to run it; cases 012--024 run with \texttt{./Allrun} and
        print a single word: \texttt{PASS}, \texttt{FAIL} or \texttt{NOTRUN}.
\end{enumerate}

Figure~\ref{fig:ladder} shows the result. The ladder climbs in four stages,
which the next sections walk through: the helium flow (001--007), moving
vapour through it (008--011), letting the vapour interact with the wall
(012--021), and finally the comparisons with experiments and with another
code (022--024). Anyone can enter at any rung, rerun it, and see the same
numbers.

