\section{The top rungs: does it match reality?}

The last three cases do not add physics; they \emph{test} it.

\textbf{022 --- reproduction of the T-Flows benchmark.} This case returns to
the temperature-model calculations in Lobresco's master's work with Bojan,
published in Ref.~\cite{Lobresco2026}, using Bojan's T-Flows code. In
``paper mode'' our model repeats their calculation: the gas level agrees
with their Fig.~9 to 0.5\,\%, and all ten lines of their Table~2 agree within
the resolution of the wall temperature table. The one visible difference,
a deposit starting 2.6\,cm earlier, disappears (to 0.9\,mm) with the
T-Flows parameter set; confirming which set generated the published figures is a specific
point to resolve with Bojan using the original T-Flows inputs. The remaining
0.9\,mm is an onset-position comparison, not proof that the entire
profiles coincide.

\textbf{023 --- against five experiments.} The five \PbI{} experiments of
Lobresco et al.~\cite{Lobresco2026} differ in helium flow (5, 106 and
540\,mL/min) and duration (6 to 300\,s). We ran all five with the HKS wall
law, the measured wall temperatures, and \emph{no parameter adjusted to the
data}, then applied the paper's own rule for \Tdep{} (the point where the
deposit reaches 1\,\% of its peak). Table~\ref{tab:023} and
Fig.~\ref{fig:bench}a show the outcome: an RMS error of 18\,K, against
about 35\,K for each of the two published model configurations. This
is a comparison of complete modelling setups, not an isolated comparison
of the OpenFOAM and T-Flows discretisations. A coarser mesh changes the
answers by at most 0.3\,K, and the mass ledger closes to $3.3\times10^{-16}$.

For a deposit profile $q(x)$, the reported onset is the first crossing
of $0.01\max q$ in the selected deposit region:
\begin{equation}
 T_{{\rm dep},r}=T_w(x_{1\%,r}),\qquad
 E_{\rm RMS}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{5}\sum_{r=1}^{5}
 (T_{{\rm dep},r}^{\rm model}-T_{{\rm dep},r}^{\rm exp})^2}.
\end{equation}
It is an onset statistic, not a peak-position or full-profile error.
The low-flow case uses the main downstream deposit, as noted below.

